---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { repo, releases, vitrina, url } from "../lib/site";

const aptSetup = `sudo install -d -m 0755 /etc/apt/keyrings
curl -fsSL https://vitrina-project.dev/vitrina.asc | sudo tee /etc/apt/keyrings/vitrina.asc > /dev/null
sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/vitrina.sources > /dev/null <<'EOF'
Types: deb
URIs: https://vitrina-project.dev/apt
Suites: stable
Components: main
Architectures: amd64 arm64
Signed-By: /etc/apt/keyrings/vitrina.asc
EOF
sudo apt update
sudo apt install jkit`;

const packages = `sudo apt install ./jkit_*_amd64.deb      # Debian, Ubuntu
sudo dnf install ./jkit-*.x86_64.rpm     # Fedora, RHEL, Rocky, Alma`;

const source = `git clone ${repo}.git
cd jkit
./mvnw verify                                   # build and test on the JVM
GRAALVM_HOME=/path/to/graalvm-25 ./mvnw -Pnative package -DskipTests
sudo install jkit-cli/target/jkit /usr/local/bin/`;

const firstSteps = `jkit search jdk
jkit install jdk@21
jkit use jdk@21 --global`;
---

<Layout title="Install jkit" description="Install jkit on Debian, Ubuntu, Fedora, RHEL or from source, then turn on the shell hook.">
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Install jkit</h1>
    <p>
      jkit is a single native binary. It needs no JDK to run. Linux packages need glibc 2.34 or
      newer: Debian 12+, Ubuntu 22.04+, Fedora, and RHEL, Rocky or Alma 9+.
    </p>

    <h2>Debian and Ubuntu: apt</h2>
    <p>
      jkit is published in the <a href={vitrina}>Vitrina</a> apt repository, so
      <code>apt upgrade</code> keeps it current. Add the repository once, then install:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{aptSetup}</code></pre>

    <h2>.deb or .rpm download</h2>
    <p>Download the package for your system from the <a href={releases}>latest release</a>, then:</p>
    <pre><code>{packages}</code></pre>

    <h2 id="from-source">From source</h2>
    <p>Building needs JDK 25 and, for the native binary, GraalVM 25.</p>
    <pre><code>{source}</code></pre>

    <h2>Turn on the shell hook</h2>
    <p>
      A program can't change the shell that started it, so <code>jkit use</code> needs a small
      shell function. Add one line to your shell's startup file:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Shell</th><th>Add to</th><th>Line</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>bash</td><td><code>~/.bashrc</code></td><td><code>eval "$(jkit hook bash)"</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>zsh</td><td><code>~/.zshrc</code></td><td><code>eval "$(jkit hook zsh)"</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>fish</td><td><code>~/.config/fish/config.fish</code></td><td><code>jkit hook fish | source</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The hook also puts the global versions on <code>PATH</code> and sets <code>JAVA_HOME</code>,
      <code>MAVEN_HOME</code>, <code>GRADLE_HOME</code> and <code>GRAALVM_HOME</code> when you
      have not set them yourself.
    </p>
    <p>
      Tab completion comes with it in bash and zsh: commands, options, tools and vendor names, so
      <code>jkit install jdk@21 cor</code> and Tab gives <code>corretto</code>. The .deb and .rpm
      packages also install it for bash on their own.
    </p>

    <h2>First steps</h2>
    <pre><code>{firstSteps}</code></pre>
    <p>Then see the <a href={url("docs")}>command reference</a>.</p>

    <p class="note">
      macOS and Windows packages are planned but not published yet. Until then, macOS users can
      build from source.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
